From c8290f9f2e1d119512e1821fc13d1d145aa23f77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Geert Uytterhoeven <geert+renesas@glider.be>
Date: Wed, 8 Feb 2017 19:00:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: r8a7743: Fix SCIFB0 dmas indentation

Fixes: 809c013426914694 ("ARM: dts: r8a7743: add [H]SCIF{A|B} support")
Signed-off-by: Geert Uytterhoeven <geert+renesas@glider.be>
Signed-off-by: Simon Horman <horms+renesas@verge.net.au>

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/r8a7743.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/r8a7743.dtsi
index d8393b97768b..c166be2f18e0 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/r8a7743.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/r8a7743.dtsi
@@ -277,7 +277,7 @@ scifb0: serial@e6c20000 {
 			clocks = <&cpg CPG_MOD 206>;
 			clock-names = "fck";
 			dmas = <&dmac0 0x3d>, <&dmac0 0x3e>,
-		       <&dmac1 0x3d>, <&dmac1 0x3e>;
+			       <&dmac1 0x3d>, <&dmac1 0x3e>;
 			dma-names = "tx", "rx", "tx", "rx";
 			power-domains = <&sysc R8A7743_PD_ALWAYS_ON>;
 			status = "disabled";
-- 
2.25.1

You are a programming expert.

You will be given the root cause of a bug.

Based on these details, your task is to determine whether the provided code snippet contains the bug.
Think step by step.
Be conservative in your assessment. If you are unsure, answer "no."

Root cause:
The bug was caused by incorrect indentation in the device tree source file, which can lead to misinterpretation of the `dmas` property by the device tree parser, potentially affecting the configuration of DMA channels for the SCIFB0 device. Proper indentation is crucial for maintaining the structure and readability of the device tree, ensuring that hardware components are correctly defined and initialized.



Now, determine whether the provided code snippet is buggy:
			clock-names = "fck";
			dmas = <&dmac0 0x3d>, <&dmac0 0x3e>,
		       <&dmac1 0x3d>, <&dmac1 0x3e>;
			dma-names = "tx", "rx", "tx", "rx";
			power-domains = <&sysc R8A7743_PD_ALWAYS_ON>;
			status = "disabled";
		};


Please output the result in the following format:
Reason: <text>
Result: <yes or no>
